 ## Background
-The concept of splatting was introduced by Westover in 1989, who proposed mapping view-dependent footprints to a generic footprint table to improve rendering speed [S2, unofficial]. Mueller and Yagel later used an image-order splatting approach to improve accuracy with perspective projections [S2, unofficial]. While early implementations used Gaussian reconstruction kernels, other kernel types such as quadratic spline functions have been proposed for higher quality images under certain conditions [S2, unofficial]. The evolution of these pipelines has been driven by the need for real-time interactivity, leading to the adoption of GPU rasterization pipelines and multi-pass architectures for handling complex lighting and transparency [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+The concept of rendering pipelines in volume visualization has evolved from early software-based ray casting to hardware-accelerated splatting and modern multi-pass deferred shading techniques. The foundational work on splatting, such as Westover's volume splatting, introduced the idea of mapping view-dependent footprints to generic tables to improve speed [S2, unofficial]. Later developments, such as the shear-warp factorization, enabled efficient hardware implementation of volume rendering pipelines [S1, unofficial]. The rise of neural radiance fields (NeRFs) and 3D Gaussian Splatting has further driven the development of modular, differentiable, and highly optimized rendering pipelines that separate scene representation from rendering logic [S1, unofficial]. These methods are widely used in real-time visualization, scientific imaging, and computer graphics training pipelines.
 
